Because GEI pillars are measured at the national level and vary primarily across countries rather than within countries over time, they cannot be identified in a two-way region-and-year fixed effects specification -- national variation would be absorbed by region fixed effects.
This is why H4 is tested in two complementary ways for robustness: (i) in fixed effects models, and (ii) in hierarchical random effects models where are regions nested within countries, where GEI variables operate at the country level as intended.


